  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Isaias bring rain, breezy conditions, \nand potential for tornadoes today.\n\nRain associated with the remnants of Hurricane Isaias has overspread \nthe entire region early this morning. Observations indicate most of \nthis is light, though a few pockets of moderate rain have been \nreported in Central VA. A few stations along the central VA Blue \nRidge have already measured over 1\" of rain, with most of Central VA \nat or just above half an inch. Winds have also started picking up in \nthe Alleghenies, with the strongest gust at 43mph in Garrett County. \n\nSynoptically not much has changed as the remnant mid-level \ncirculation of Isaias is now a large cutoff low over the TN Valley. \nForcing for ascent over the Mid-Atlantic is helping increase the \ncoverage of rain. The interaction of the cutoff low to the west and \nsurface high to the east will create a large area of convergence \n/ broad frontal zone over the Mid-Atlantic. SPC Mesoanalysis \nindicates that PWATs of 2\" are surging north into our area from NC \nand southern VA.\n\nRain totals are generally still around 1.5-2.5\" across the area. \nOrographic effects of easterly winds into the Blue Ridge will result \nin 3-4\" of rain along the eastern slopes into the Piedmont. The \nFlood Watch in effect for those areas continues. Some of the high \nres models indicate the potential for enhanced rainfall over the DC \nmetro late this evening as a surface wave of low pressure approaches \nthen slows right around DC, before quickly departing to the east \nlate tonight. Any additional rain in the metro area could certainly \nlead to flooding concerns, so will continue to monitor model and \nradar trends to see if the Flood Watch need to be expanded. \n\nIn terms of the severe weather threat, SPC has expanded the Slight \nRisk into most of Central VA, with a Marginal up into the I-81 \ncorridor to the MD/PA border. Once the main shield of rain departs \nto the north this afternoon, most of the area enters a brief lull \nwhere we could see some breaks in the clouds. There's a better \nchance for this to occur over Central VA to Southern MD. A wave of \nsurface low pressure then tracks through the area this afternoon. If \nthis can take advantage of any present instability, there is more \nthan enough shear to fuel organized severe convection. Model \nsoundings still show elongated hodographs, so the tornado threat is \ngoing to be front and center, though damaging wind gusts are also \npossible. The biggest question mark remains how much instability can \ndevelop, and how far north that instability will reach. Right now, \nit appears the most favorable area is going to be east of the Blue \nRidge and south of Route 50. \n\nThe Wind Advisory remains through late this morning for the \nAlleghenies and Blue Ridge where gusts of 45-50 mph are expected. \nElsewhere, breezy conditions develop late this morning into the \nafternoon, with the strongest gusts around 20-30 mph. \n\nAnother round of steady rain is likely tonight as the cutoff low \naloft moves across the Mid-Atlantic. Precip comes to an end from \nwest to east Monday morning as the remnants of Isaias finally push \noffshore. Humid and warm conditions for Monday afternoon, though \nmost of the area is going to be dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures through mid week, then\ncooler conditions as moisture starved fronts move through.\n\nBrief ridging may bring high temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday\nto near 80 degrees. Low pressure passing north of the area will\nsend a cold front southward sometime on Wednesday. Most of the \nrain likely remains across Pennsylvania, although some showers \nmay sneak southward. \n\nThere remains significant model spread the progression of\nadditional fronts and upper troughs at the end of this week into\nnext weekend. However, the trend remains for cooler conditions\nas highs return closer to normal values for Thursday and\nFriday.",
